GNU make for OpenVMS
====================

This is GNU make @VERSION@ built natively for OpenVMS @ARCH@, from the
unmodified GNU release (whose own OpenVMS port it uses) plus the patches in
the vms-make project:

    https://github.com/issinoho/vms-make

Kit version: @KITVERSION@.  GNU make is free software under the GNU General
Public License, version 3 or later; see COPYING.  The manual is MAKE.TXT;
MAKE.1 is the manual page.  README_UPSTREAM.VMS is GNU make's own notes on
its OpenVMS port: read it for how makefiles behave when make runs from DCL.

What is installed
-----------------
    MAKE$ROOT:[BIN]MAKE.EXE             make
    MAKE$ROOT:[000000]MAKE$SETUP.COM    defines the make and gmake commands
    MAKE$ROOT:[DOC]                     this file, README_UPSTREAM.VMS,
                                        COPYING, NEWS, MAKE.TXT, MAKE.1
    SYS$STARTUP:MAKE$STARTUP.COM        defines MAKE$ROOT

Setting up
----------
The kit installs MAKE$STARTUP.COM into SYS$STARTUP and runs it once.  To
define MAKE$ROOT at every boot, the system manager adds this line to
SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM:

    $ @SYS$STARTUP:MAKE$STARTUP.COM

Each user (or SYLOGIN.COM, for everyone) then defines the commands:

    $ @MAKE$ROOT:[000000]MAKE$SETUP.COM

Makefiles on VMS
----------------
Run from DCL, make runs each recipe line as a DCL command in a subprocess.
Makefiles are written for VMS: recipes are DCL commands, and a "$" that
DCL should see (SYS$OUTPUT, F$SEARCH) is written "$$", as in any makefile.

    out.txt : in.txt
            copy in.txt out.txt

    show :
            write sys$$output "built $(words a b c) things"

make looks for MAKEFILE.VMS, GNUMAKEFILE and MAKEFILE, in that order.  The
details (comma-separated file lists, GNV$ settings, built-in rules for
VMS) are in README_UPSTREAM.VMS.

Things that differ from Unix
----------------------------
- Capturing make's output.  Run make at the terminal, in a batch job, with
  PIPE (PIPE make > make.log) or with SPAWN/OUTPUT=make.log.  Do not
  redirect it with DEFINE/USER SYS$OUTPUT: the subprocesses that run the
  recipes cannot write to that file and fail with %DCL-W-UNDFIL.

- Quote upper-case options and variable assignments ("CC=cc"), or SET
  PROCESS/PARSE_STYLE=EXTENDED: with the default TRADITIONAL parse style
  DCL changes the case of unquoted arguments.  Batch jobs use TRADITIONAL
  even when interactive logins are set to EXTENDED.

- Exit status.  Under DCL a failed make exits with an error severity, so
  ON ERROR and IF .NOT. $STATUS work.
